In the meantime, here’s a quick guide to economy class for your routes:
- Fare types
  - Basic Economy/Light: Lowest price; usually no or limited advance seat selection, last to board, changes often restricted or come with higher fees; checked bag policies vary; earning miles sometimes reduced.
  - Standard/Main Cabin: More flexible; advance seat selection included, changes allowed (fare difference usually applies); better mileage earning.
- Baggage (transatlantic varies by airline/fare)
  - Carry-on: Typically 1 carry-on + 1 personal item included.
  - Checked bags: Some fares include 1st checked bag on transatlantic; others charge. Standard/Main Cabin more likely to include; Basic/Light often does not. Always verify for your exact fare.
- Onboard experience (typical long-haul economy)
  - Seat pitch ~30–32 in; width ~17–18 in; blanket/pillow on most carriers; seatback entertainment, USB/AC power increasingly common; full meal service and soft drinks included; alcohol varies by airline.
- Seat selection and fees
  - Standard/Main often lets you pick regular seats for free; extra-legroom seats cost more. Basic may auto-assign or charge for any selection.
- Changes, cancellations, and upgrades
  - Standard/Main: No change fees on many carriers, but fare difference applies; refunds depend on fare rules.
  - Upgrades to premium economy/business may be available for a fee or miles; best availability early or close to departure.
- Earning miles/points
  - You’ll earn miles; accrual rate depends on fare class and program. Basic may earn less.

If you confirm the year and preferences, I’ll pull live economy options and highlight the best, cheapest, and fastest itineraries for both of you.

